$ Complete symmetry breaking compares x with transform(p,x) using .<= , which orders its
$ two sides by a representation-specific key. The right hand side becomes an auxiliary,
$ so the auxiliary has to be given the same representation as x -- two different keys are
$ not comparable, and comparing them keeps or loses whole orbits.
$
$ Whichever representations conjure picks, the answer has to be the same: the 9 partial
$ functions on 2 points fall into 6 orbits under Sym(e) -- the empty function, the two
$ one-point functions, and the three total ones. Before this was fixed, the models
$ disagreed with each other, reporting 1, 6 and 7.
letting e be new type of size 2

find x : function e --> e
